---
/**
 * THE PERSONAL CHAPTER.
 *
 * Rebuilt: photographs sit beside their text and alternate sides, instead of a
 * vertical column of full-width images. Same photographs, same rule — each one
 * whole, alone, never cropped, never two in a frame together.
 *
 * The résumé facts live inside this prose rather than in a CV block, because
 * the portfolio IS the résumé.
 */
import Aside from '../components/Aside.astro';
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro';

import iAmWhoIAm from '../assets/aura/06-i-am-who-i-am.jpg';
import lobbyBlazer from '../assets/photos/03-lobby-blazer.jpg';
import mountains from '../assets/photos/04-mountains.jpg';
import wallBw from '../assets/photos/06-wall-bw.jpg';
import jumping from '../assets/photos/08-jumping.jpg';
import gym from '../assets/photos/09-gym-back.jpg';
import barneyAwesome from '../assets/aura/08-barney-awesome.jpg';
---

<section class="hu section" id="who">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="label hu__k">Enough about the code</p>
    <h2 class="hu__t display">Who's actually building this</h2>

    <Aside src={lobbyBlazer} side="left"
      alt="Shaurya Verma standing in a hotel lobby in a light grey blazer and glasses, hands in his pockets, smiling."
      caption="The one my mother would pick.">
      <p>
        Final year, Computer Science, Thapar Institute in Patiala — currently a <strong>7.62 CGPA</strong>. Before
        that: 90% in Class XII and <strong>98.6% in Class X</strong>, and an <strong>NTSE scholarship</strong> from
        the Government of India, the aptitude exam that takes roughly the top 0.1% nationally.
      </p>
      <p>
        I mention the NTSE rather than the CGPA when someone asks what I'm good at, because one of those measures
        how fast I pick up a thing I've never seen and the other measures how well I sat through semesters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two first places at IIT Ropar — the Gen AI Hackathon in November 2025 with a multimodal Alzheimer's
        detection system, which became Sehat Saarthi, and the Agentic &amp; GenAI Showcase in July 2026 with
        Sifarish — plus the AI Mentorship Programme with LaunchED Global, run out of IIT Kharagpur.
      </p>
    </Aside>

    <Aside src={mountains} side="right"
      alt="Shaurya Verma standing on a Himalayan ridge in a white polo shirt and sunglasses, both thumbs up, a forested valley and layered mountain ranges behind him."
      caption="Uttarakhand.">
      <p>
        I want to do something genuinely big. Not "senior engineer by thirty" big — I mean point myself at a
        problem that matters and actually move it. Right now that means getting inside a team building frontier AI
        systems and being useful there faster than anyone expects a final-year student to be.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of the projects on this site were assigned to me. I go looking for problems; it's close to
        compulsive. I see something broken and some part of my brain starts chewing on it until the thing that
        fixes it exists.
      </p>
    </Aside>

    <Aside src={wallBw} side="left"
      alt="Shaurya Verma in front of a wall covered in printed photographs and quote cards, photographed in black and white, laughing."
      caption="The wall, again.">
      <p>
        Scroll back to the top for a second. The wall behind me in that first photograph is the wall in my room,
        and every image on this site that isn't a photograph of me or a screenshot of my work is printed and stuck
        on it — the Stark frames, the SRK line, Barney, the blackletter one.
      </p>
      <p>
        They went up long before there was a portfolio to put them in. That's why they're here instead of some
        tasteful abstract pattern: I didn't choose them for a website, I chose them for a wall I look at every
        morning.
      </p>
    </Aside>

    <Aside src={jumping} side="right"
      alt="Shaurya Verma mid-jump on his bed with both arms thrown up, laughing, the wall of printed images behind him."
      caption="A normal Tuesday.">
      <p>
        My friends come to me for two things: motivation, and consolation. Those are opposite jobs and I'm somehow
        the person for both, which I think says something reasonably good about me.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want someone who makes the workplace more fun, I'm available for that too. I'd like the place I
        work to be somewhere I'm allowed to be good company.
      </p>
    </Aside>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Aside src={gym} side="left"
      alt="Shaurya Verma in a bathroom mirror, flexing, his back visible in the reflection behind him."
      caption="The other discipline.">
      <p>
        I lift. Not as a personality, just as the other thing I am strict about — the gym is the only place I
        know where the feedback loop is completely honest. The weight moves or it does not. Nothing about your
        intent matters. You find out what you actually did, every single session.
      </p>
      <p>
        I think that is the same instinct that runs through everything on this site. I would rather be told the
        truth by a system than flattered by one, and I build the same way: put the measurement in, let it tell
        me I was wrong, and change the thing.
      </p>
      <p class="hu__vol">
        Off-campus I volunteer with <strong>Bharat Vikas Parishad</strong>, and on campus I have sat on the core
        organising committee for <strong>Saturnalia</strong> — Thapar's techno-cultural festival — and on the
        committee for <strong>Urja</strong>, the sports festival. Running a festival is the least glamorous
        project-management training available and easily the most useful.
      </p>
    </Aside>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Aside src={iAmWhoIAm} side="left"
      alt="A silhouetted figure and an outstretched hand rendered entirely in streaks of starlight, captioned with Exodus 3:14 — I AM WHO I AM."
      caption="Exodus 3:14, rendered in starlight.">
      <p>
        I don't spend much energy being a version of myself that is easier to categorise. It has cost me
        nothing so far and it saves an enormous amount of time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The short version: I am loud about the work and quiet about almost everything else, I would rather be
        wrong in public than vague in private, and I have never once been the most qualified person in a room
        and let that decide the outcome.
      </p>
    </Aside>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Aside src={barneyAwesome} side="right"
      alt="Neil Patrick Harris reclining at a desk in a suit, photographed in black and white, captioned: I'm sorry, I can't hear you over the sound of how awesome I am.">
      <p>
        I am aware of how all of this looks. That is rather the point — I would rather be memorable and slightly
        ridiculous than forgettable and safe.
      </p>
      <p>
        But every claim on this site has a link under it going to the code that proves it. The swagger is the
        packaging. The receipts are the product.
      </p>
    </Aside>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hu__k { color: var(--gold); }
  .hu__t { font-size: var(--t-title); margin-top: var(--s-4); }
  .hu__aura { margin-block: clamp(var(--s-12), 8vh, var(--s-24)); }

  .hu__ak { color: var(--gold); }
  .hu__at { font-size: var(--t-head); margin-top: var(--s-2); color: var(--star); }

  .hu__play {
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--s-3);
    padding: 12px 24px; min-height: 44px;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--gold) 8%, transparent);
    border: var(--hair) solid var(--gold);
    border-radius: var(--r-full);
    color: var(--gold); font-size: var(--t-small); cursor: pointer;
    transition: background var(--d-base) var(--ease);
  }
  .hu__play:hover { background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--gold) 18%, transparent); }
  .hu__playicon {
    width: 0; height: 0; flex: none;
    border-left: 9px solid currentColor;
    border-top: 6px solid transparent; border-bottom: 6px solid transparent;
  }
  .hu__play[data-on='true'] .hu__playicon {
    width: 9px; height: 12px; border: none;
    background: linear-gradient(to right, currentColor 0 3px, transparent 3px 6px, currentColor 6px 9px);
  }
  .hu__anote { margin-top: var(--s-4); font-size: var(--t-micro); color: var(--dust); }
  .hu__vol { font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--dust); }
  .hu__vol strong { color: var(--star-dim); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
